# Mutation testing per package: <package> <efficacy floor %> <killed mutants floor>.
#
# The ratchet is the KILLED COUNT, because it is the number that cannot fall without an assertion
# being lost. Efficacy is killed over reachable mutants, so covering new code brings new mutants
# into the denominator and *lowers* the ratio while nothing gets worse — control has done that
# twice, going 84.56% -> 84.42% on one pass and 88.54% -> 86.23% on the next, with killed rising
# 126 -> 130 -> 139 -> 144 each time. A floor on the ratio alone would be red for the right work.
#
# So: the efficacy floor may sit below the highest observation and tolerates that dip; the killed
# floor may only move up, and it is checked separately.
#
# Measured 2026-10-27.
internal/control     94  169
internal/sandbox    100  409
internal/supervise   87   47
internal/update      90  256
internal/vault       95  114
internal/workerpool  87    7
internal/ready       86   31
#
# What a survivor means, read from control's last sweep (169 killed, 10 lived):
#   six are log-only conditions — auth.go:32, auth.go:35, turn.go:269, workspaces.go:1006,
#     workspaces.go:1040 twice. Each one decides whether to write a line, and killing it means
#     asserting on a log string, which pins wording instead of behaviour;
#   three are boundaries on a key that cannot repeat — workspaces.go:316 and :983 sort workspace
#     names, models.go:578 compares scale ranks, and both are unique by construction, so `<` and
#     `<=` are the same function;
#   one is the two-minute budget on turn.go:267, which is only observable by waiting two minutes.
#
# vault's four are the same shape: two sort comparators on secret names (vault.go:178, :180, and
# :637 twice), and a name is unique in a list of secrets by definition, so `<` and `<=` are one
# function. Its fifth is a time-out: the mutation makes the store keep its lock, and gremlins
# reports that as timed out rather than killed.
# Those are the honest end of a mutation score, not a gap in the tests.
#
# Not measured, on purpose: `internal/httpapi` and `cmd/acornfall` keep their tests in the
# end-to-end suite, and gremlins only runs the package's own. A number from them would report the
# absence of unit tests, not the strength of the tests. Their signal is coverage through the
# feature suite, and `make cover` says it.
